#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "945f684f-9707-5a0a-b5d7-0753f11b4385" :question/math-academy-id "e-5266" :question/problem "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "7e876c7b-849f-59ce-a654-881ec9464475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5269" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-1) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124365 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/id #uuid "5ce5b329-ac5d-5581-b396-eadd569b9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-6087" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1\n$$\n\nand the plane $x=2$  onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2,$ we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}(y+5)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9abffec8-59b6-56e3-a85a-7decbc2ec544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381302} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4851fd-0030-5593-a93a-8692d84d61ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381303} {:answer/id #uuid "16efb8d3-089e-54d0-8f6d-1e5156bde1a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381304} {:answer/id #uuid "4639e838-770f-5aae-8aae-fecdcebbd840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381305} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaeb6d7-1b2f-52c0-8058-124dbba7c657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16efb8d3-089e-54d0-8f6d-1e5156bde1a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381304} :answer-field/id #uuid "031332fa-2d50-5b6a-8fd8-4dfda94e1dfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e42cf59-2800-542c-8376-eb0ff45df241" :question/math-academy-id "q-164264" :question/problem "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $z-5=3(x-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-5 & =3(x-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} \\\\ z-5 & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1/3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{1/2} \\\\ z-5 & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1/3}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(2,-1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73016fa2-ecca-594b-9202-2f6021575d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282674} {:answer/id #uuid "9090c86c-b4ff-5fce-87cc-c598e3bbe682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282939} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c270e1-3d1b-5446-a44f-56f2d24ed916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283409} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dbbd98-fa3b-5fb4-9198-459917b88e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283412} {:answer/id #uuid "c934f983-21d3-5225-b0d3-a9027347c30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c934f983-21d3-5225-b0d3-a9027347c30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283447} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb3e580-657b-5cc5-854e-3f005e11ce16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f605c5a-bdb8-58e6-a4e2-fd64274d89f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164273" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+4 & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(3,-1,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "593952d1-603f-5697-b907-5540e353de04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491621} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4f4be7-060b-56f0-97f2-a38d339ecc2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491655} {:answer/id #uuid "9c77ee9d-084d-5e55-82fd-5f2296dd96bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491726} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc8c3e5-1e99-5aaa-b702-9c9113341792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491803} {:answer/id #uuid "da4c09e0-be8a-5958-94e9-6bce9a534264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc8c3e5-1e99-5aaa-b702-9c9113341792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491803} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe8f42e-bd52-5557-a69d-1be3b48f9022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46ec68bc-6534-5a9b-ab9f-a1364685c87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35557" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-(-1) & =\\frac{(x-(-3))^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-3,0,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa6485c-6c26-5611-afdf-703d5d2c4fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402443} {:answer/id #uuid "94e8800d-2d94-543f-9790-e394e9982e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 3(x+2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402444} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8e1f20-aacc-5193-b98b-3863146e7855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $x=3(z\\pm 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402445} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea80951-99ce-5495-a089-c32574e56f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=27(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402446} {:answer/id #uuid "517d88bc-5b1b-5eeb-90dd-f07f46cd9dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa6485c-6c26-5611-afdf-703d5d2c4fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402443} :answer-field/id #uuid "adcc4a54-ddf6-5374-9d97-b2333d8e09c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69276016-ba7c-50d9-a595-a7d6a823a97c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35577" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f518821-8d54-51fc-9bce-f3a0f5f589ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121983} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a4221a-5cb4-5e5a-91d9-0ebc20721597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121984} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d70452-4d0c-587a-80d4-f421a69093c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121985} {:answer/id #uuid "d209bcdd-0867-5a87-b48b-a81619d58794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121986} {:answer/id #uuid "3a117682-1229-54ea-8960-cf79d7b9dbfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121987}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dca9a97c-39eb-5182-b64c-f7c2b0694e92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121982}] :question/id #uuid "5f9b5701-5a46-5b44-acab-e449b1e6d7a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-35703" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x - 1)^{2} + 25(y + 1)^{2} = 100(z - 2)$ and the plane\n$z = 7$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22da1385-6266-5799-b4e6-1450ac000f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402477} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbba806-2c60-5f4a-baa8-3853daf14f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402478} {:answer/id #uuid "2a71a09f-4e8f-5184-8408-6df063f93f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402479} {:answer/id #uuid "25eabda5-e7e6-5265-8753-b7375e5072b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402480} {:answer/id #uuid "18dd6731-d299-5fcc-a868-dcd21e9a1695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a71a09f-4e8f-5184-8408-6df063f93f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402479} :answer-field/id #uuid "5075bff5-d814-5717-a7ce-09c9a7f1c710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c2420eb-a369-5201-ac95-195a1c7fe310" :question/math-academy-id "q-35906" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=z+2$ and the plane \n$y=3$  onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of this parabola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $y=3$, we substitute $y=3$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-1 & =z+2 \\\\ z+3 & =\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at $(-1,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205d1a65-04f6-59a3-a974-5b778afaa91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321391} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcfa493-b877-5976-8c32-70d3d57ac828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321760} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e9f3f-adaa-5df3-bb70-cd9f70742907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321898} {:answer/id #uuid "e24be124-29de-5243-8c6e-8306d6f59e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322915} {:answer/id #uuid "eccdcbac-973f-5f39-80bc-76abea742d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e9f3f-adaa-5df3-bb70-cd9f70742907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321898} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f3b7e6-356f-5e12-ac50-e4c8413a78be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04c0f5ae-99dd-5b51-8d08-843351e49a3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-35930" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2}=z$ and the plane $z=2$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. Find the center of this ellipse." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=2$, we substitute $z=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =z \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2/5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{2/3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse in the $xy$-plane that has its center at $(-2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5f1447-ab77-58cb-8126-759c5f38ff4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3,0)$ and $(0,5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336324} {:answer/id #uuid "72bd4180-03f6-5737-a5eb-b34f6b442054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337062} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4e832f-6bcf-5dfe-948c-1e1f4e3fd789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337161} {:answer/id #uuid "84cf1df2-2b31-53a8-a319-d20ca48f2922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337230} {:answer/id #uuid "b5259df7-f023-5598-a067-611c99d1afb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,0)$ and $(0,5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72bd4180-03f6-5737-a5eb-b34f6b442054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337062} :answer-field/id #uuid "61cf01d0-b238-55cd-b14f-c71058799ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67f91642-80cb-5303-9cbd-7565e0fd0fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72191" :question/problem "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+13=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+13 & =\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{25}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =5+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=3,-5.$ \n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c97666-a893-5f35-bc7c-50e394cdf2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,5,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336907} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddee09f-5f73-5f51-bea7-9d1662201b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337006} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a92176-77b8-5361-83f7-4198ec1afc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337483} {:answer/id #uuid "a28e586f-fbfc-5bc8-99af-e45e1eec08d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337508} {:answer/id #uuid "b8015139-3877-5c1f-ba59-0e5c12a15c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8015139-3877-5c1f-ba59-0e5c12a15c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337662} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cea0b39-2b87-534b-bcae-9ae2b0cbfcc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ca33d54-3052-5d14-952c-2bb3467296fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-72301" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+2=\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+2 & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =\\frac{(x-(-5))^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-5,2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4441e7-9ebc-5545-9efa-2ee1440fa1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366235} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc6b114-2ad4-5286-98ef-bab6af5d1a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366236} {:answer/id #uuid "2b746e61-be9d-59cb-8bf8-3b68b34626f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366237} {:answer/id #uuid "e590b348-2055-5203-8c91-c618c41626da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366238} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4e2c-448d-5b40-8003-6a09149d5ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b746e61-be9d-59cb-8bf8-3b68b34626f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366237} :answer-field/id #uuid "98778e41-d388-55c7-822d-6a1e70584ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16658c9e-8fa3-5c30-87d5-30c0a6e7db83" :question/math-academy-id "q-72302" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x-6)^{2}-8(y+2)^{2}=z+4$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-6)^{2}-8(y+2)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ 36-8(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ 32 & =8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =-2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=-4$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5610de-403e-5562-a4a7-ca6322eccb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339371} {:answer/id #uuid "23d97d0d-cfb5-54ba-9b4f-62c7c7656fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339440} {:answer/id #uuid "28c48720-2495-559a-aab4-2755bd478193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339486} {:answer/id #uuid "4d95a679-cb0d-51af-9831-4ac9632939cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339836} {:answer/id #uuid "96506ef6-052b-5dd0-a31d-dfb184a2409b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c5610de-403e-5562-a4a7-ca6322eccb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339371} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9395dc4-45a6-50d8-8f4e-87541ed3e2aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d959e918-e9be-5939-a2a7-015f4bbb6f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73862" :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-2,4,3).$"}]}
